ence of the small pairs. If the opponents gain the lead, they are likely to play high cards or combinations that the Landlord cannot defend against with 77 or 88. Consequently, these pairs risk becoming "dead cards" stuck in the hand.\n\nReason: Leading the pair of 7s is the best move to mitigate risk. It immediately removes two low-ranking cards from the hand. Since the Landlord lacks high pairs (K, A, 2) to regain the lead on a pair track, this is likely the only opportunity to play the 7s (and potentially the 8s if the 7s pass). Leading the single 4 is dangerous because opponents can easily win the lead with intermediate cards, leaving the Landlord trapped with difficult-to-play small pairs.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥7", "♦7"]}'}
